WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:03.470
- [Kim] Civil society
is one of those terms

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.690
that you might hear in
a politician's speech.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:09.710
Maybe in a line about the
importance of maintaining

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:11.570
a strong relationship between

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:14.220
the government and civil society.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.900
But what does it actually mean?

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.500
A society that's civilized?

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.810
Or maybe the part of
society run by civilians

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.230
rather than government agents?

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:29.180
In fact civil society is
a little hard to define.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.550
Let's start with what it's not.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.250
It's not government or elected officials

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.050
who are acting in their official capacity

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.090
as representatives of the state or nation.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.260
That is the political sphere.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.020
It's also not businesses or corporations

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:50.240
who exist primarily to make a profit,

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.550
that's the economic sphere.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.530
And lastly, it's not purely
private aspects of society,

00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:58.723
such as family or home life.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.930
Civil society exists in
between these three spheres

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:07.890
and includes all of the
non-governmental groups,

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.610
associations, and institutions

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.660
that civilians form and join.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.720
If you and your friends decide to create

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.840
a neighborhood cleanup club,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.630
picking up litter from the streets

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.450
and planting trees in the park,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.970
congratulations, you've
just joined civil society.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.070
Some other examples of
civil society include

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:33.580
religious organizations,
professional associations,

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.600
charitable groups, or interest groups.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.120
Non-governmental organizations or NGOs

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.660
are some of the most notable
actors in civil society.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:48.400
These include organizations
like Doctors Without Borders.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.010
Some organizations overlap
a bit with other spheres.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:56.460
Labor unions, for example,
are voluntary organizations

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.880
but they overlap with the economic sphere

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.900
by petitioning businesses for better wages

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.060
and working conditions.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.420
NGOs that have a humanitarian mission,

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.680
like Amnesty International

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.150
may overlap with the political sphere,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.720
since they petition governments to change

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.890
how they treat their citizens.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.600
Some non-governmental actors
are harder to classify

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.250
like the news media.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.270
Where does it fall in civil society?

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:28.260
It usually has some goal of making money

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.570
but it also helps inform citizens

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.200
about the workings of government

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:35.460
and helps hold politicians
accountable to the public.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.123
So the media plays a central
role in civil society.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.170
So what's important about civil society?

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:45.850
Well all of these organizations

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.960
help connect people to their communities

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.700
and many of them strive to improve society

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.530
by influencing these spheres.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.490
Often they're in conflict with one another

00:02:57.490 --> 00:02:59.000
but most importantly they show us

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.330
that there are many ways beyond voting

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.270
or running for office

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.833
to become involved in
issues that you care about.

